---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Common Limitations - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="common-limitations">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				AI coding tools are genuinely useful, but have some notable limitations. If you use them more like a fast junior
				developer, they become much easier to work with.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="success" title="Calibrating expectations">
				<p>
					For development work, AI is now roughly at the level of a <strong>new grad developer</strong>: it can complete real tasks, write functional code, and move quickly,
					but it will regularly miss nuance, overbuild, misread intent, and make
					confident mistakes. It also starts every task without your team's domain
					knowledge or the full context behind past decisions unless you provide
					that context explicitly.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<p>
				Knowing the common failure modes matters because most AI-assisted
				development problems are not caused by a total lack of ability. They're
				caused by the model being <em>almost</em> capable enough, which makes bad
				output look more trustworthy than it should.
			</p>

			<h3>Where Models Commonly Struggle</h3>

			<ul class="space-y-4 my-4">
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Hallucinated APIs, packages, and methods.</span>{" "}
					Models confidently generate code that references methods, libraries, and
					configuration options that simply do not exist or are from older versions of the library. Examples include invented
					Prisma methods like <code>prisma.$upload</code>, fabricated fetch options
					like <code>autoRetry: true</code>, and packages like{" "}
					<code>@prisma/cache</code> that were never published.{" "}
					<a href="https://31daysofvibecoding.com/2026/01/14/when-ai-hallucinates/" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800 text-sm ml-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[ref]</a>{" "}
					This happens because the model predicts plausible-looking patterns from
					training data without any ability to verify them. The code often looks
					correct at a glance, and these errors only surface at runtime.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Outdated training data and library versions.</span>{" "}
					Every model has a knowledge cutoff. It does not know about the version of
					a library you installed last week, and it will confidently suggest
					deprecated APIs, old patterns, or syntax from previous major versions.
					You will notice this most with libraries that have had recent major
					version changes. When you hit this, the fix is to link the model directly
					to the current documentation, or better yet, write or download a skill
					for that library so it has accurate reference material every time. Without
					this, you will spend more time debugging the model's outdated suggestions
					than you would writing the code yourself.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Silent failures and subtly wrong code.</span>{" "}
					This is one of the more dangerous failure modes. Newer models have gotten
					good at producing code that runs without errors, passes a quick visual
					check, and appears to work, but does not actually do what was intended.{" "}
					<a href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/ai-coding-degrades" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800 text-sm ml-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[ref]</a>{" "}
					A rate limiter config that silently ignores a setting, a filter that
					misses an edge case, a calculation that returns a plausible but wrong
					number. These bugs slip past code review because the code <em>looks</em>{" "}
					reasonable. This is why manual testing remains essential. You need to
					actually click through the feature, try the edge cases, and verify the
					behavior yourself. Automated tests help, but they only catch what you
					thought to test for. Silent failures are, by definition, the things
					nobody expected to break.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Gaming the check instead of solving the problem.</span>{" "}
					When you give a model a target like "make the tests pass" or "get the build green", it will sometimes satisfy the letter of that
					instruction without doing the real work. It might weaken or delete a failing test, hardcode the expected value, wrap the failure in a{" "}
					<code>try/catch</code> that swallows it, or call <code>sys.exit(0)</code> so the test runner reports success.{" "}
					<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/emergent-misalignment-reward-hacking" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800 text-sm ml-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[ref]</a>{" "}
					Researchers call this reward hacking, and it is different from a silent failure: the model is not confused, it has found a shortcut to
					the goal you defined. Watch for tests that changed when you only asked for a fix, suspiciously narrow assertions, and a green run that
					does not match what you see in the app.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Security vulnerabilities.</span>{" "}
					AI-generated code introduces security flaws at a surprisingly high rate, and this has not improved as models have gotten smarter.
					Veracode's Spring 2026 update re-tested the current flagship models (GPT-5.x, Gemini 3, Claude 4.5/4.6) and still found a security flaw
					in 45% of generated code, essentially unchanged from two years earlier despite large gains in functional ability.{" "}
					<a href="https://www.veracode.com/blog/spring-2026-genai-code-security/" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800 text-sm ml-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[ref]</a>{" "}
					Reasoning-focused models did somewhat better (around a 70% pass rate), but that still leaves roughly one in three snippets flawed.
					Models tend to skip input validation, use insecure defaults, and miss authentication edge cases. Treat any AI-generated code that
					handles user input, authentication, or data storage as needing explicit security review.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">UI implementation and design translation.</span>{" "}
					Models can build workable interfaces, but they are still weak at
					translating polished designs into production-quality UI. Even when you
					hook a tool into Figma, most design files are not structured cleanly
					enough to map directly into good code. Spacing rules are inconsistent,
					component intent is implicit, responsive behavior is underspecified, and
					visual polish often gets lost in translation. This is an area where you will need to do the work yourself to ensure the UI is polished and functional.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Requirement interpretation.</span> Models are
					prone to filling in missing details on their own. If a requirement is
					ambiguous, they will often choose a direction confidently instead of
					stopping to ask the question a careful developer would ask first. You can mitigate this by explicitly asking the model to ask clarifying questions before writing the code.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Agreeing with you (sycophancy).</span>{" "}
					Models are trained on human feedback, which nudges them toward telling you what you want to hear. Ask "is this a good approach?" and the
					default answer skews toward yes. Suggest a wrong fix and the model may adopt it rather than correct you.{" "}
					<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sycophancy_(artificial_intelligence)" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800 text-sm ml-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[ref]</a>{" "}
					Leading questions get unreliable answers, so ask "what are the tradeoffs of X versus Y?" rather than "X is better, right?", and treat
					enthusiastic agreement with your own idea as the weakest form of validation.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Consistency across larger changes.</span>{" "}
					Small tasks often go well. Multi-file refactors, architecture changes,
					and cross-cutting updates are where quality drops faster. The model may
					fix eight places and miss the ninth, or update code but forget tests,
					docs, migrations, or monitoring.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Judgment and taste.</span> Models can
					reproduce common patterns, but they still struggle with the kind of
					taste-based decisions strong engineers make: when to keep something
					simple, when to abstract, when a UI feels off, or when a technically
					correct solution is the wrong tradeoff for the team.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Current state, not permanent state">
				<p>
					These limitations describe the current state of AI tooling during
					development. They will improve over time, and some will improve quickly.
					But for now, the safe assumption is that the model can help execute
					work, not replace product judgment, domain expertise, or careful human
					review.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Further Reading</h3>
			<ul class="text-sm space-y-2 my-4">
				<li>
					<a href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/ai-coding-degrades" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AI Coding Assistants Are Getting Worse</a> — IEEE Spectrum, Jan 2026
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://www.veracode.com/blog/spring-2026-genai-code-security/" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring 2026 GenAI Code Security Update</a> — Veracode, Mar 2026
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://31daysofvibecoding.com/2026/01/14/when-ai-hallucinates/" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">When AI Starts Hallucinating</a> — 31 Days of Vibe Coding, Jan 2026
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://www.sonarsource.com/resources/library/llm-code-generation/" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LLMs for Code Generation: Research on Quality</a> — Sonar
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2025/08/07/create-ai-code-security-risks/" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AI Can Write Your Code, but Nearly Half May Be Insecure</a> — Help Net Security, 2025
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/emergent-misalignment-reward-hacking" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">From Shortcuts to Sabotage: Reward Hacking</a> — Anthropic, Nov 2025
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sycophancy_(artificial_intelligence)" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sycophancy in AI</a> — overview of the phenomenon and the 2025 GPT-4o incident
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
